1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,420
It was so valued, it helped turn the tide in the American Revolution.

2
00:00:07,660 --> 00:00:10,160
Napoleon knew his fortunes would sour without it.

3
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:14,760
From fields of cane to acres of beets,

4
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,920
it's processed into a multi-billion dollar wonder.

5
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:20,820
It not only satisfies the world's sweet tooth,

6
00:00:21,260 --> 00:00:24,740
but one day might even replace our dependence on oil.

7
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:28,720
Now, Sugar on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:48,923 --> 00:00:52,240
Today, the average American consumes 43 pounds a year.

9
00:00:52,241 --> 00:00:58,240
Down under, Australians eat 65 pounds each.

10
00:00:58,241 --> 00:01:10,640
Sugar is produced in 121 countries, and annual production is over 150 million tons.

11
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:18,220
Like oil or grain, sugar is one of a handful of substances that makes the world go round.

12
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:23,772
We certainly have a sweet tooth. Sugar is a source of pleasure.

13
00:01:23,773 --> 00:01:25,980
It takes an enormous amount

14
00:01:25,981 --> 00:01:30,320
of effort to produce, and we're willing to spend that

15
00:01:30,321 --> 00:01:32,980
to maintain that pleasure in our lives.

16
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:39,880
For thousands of years, the Hawaiian Islands have been home

17
00:01:39,881 --> 00:01:41,780
to one of the world's most treasured goods.

18
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:47,820
Here in Maui, the environment for growing sugar is ideal.

19
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:50,500
Sugarcane is a tropical grass,

20
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:53,800
so it's going to yield best in a tropical environment.

21
00:01:53,801 --> 00:01:58,800
We're at about 20 degrees north on the globe, so it's an optimum place to grow sugarcane.

22
00:01:58,801 --> 00:02:04,800
Endlessly renewable, sugarcane is grown by planting clones, not seeds.

23
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:13,800
We cut the existing adult plant into pieces, very similar to a potato.

24
00:02:13,801 --> 00:02:18,800
This is the eye of the sugarcane plant, and this new plant will grow out of this eye.

25
00:02:18,801 --> 00:02:23,480
In most areas, sugar cane is grown and harvested on an annual rotation.

26
00:02:25,620 --> 00:02:30,600
But in Hawaii, the hybrid cane they've developed takes two years to fully mature.

27
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:36,700
That results in high sugar content and distinctive stalks that reach as high as 30 feet.

28
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:43,360
At two years, the crops have reached their maximum sugar saturation.

29
00:02:43,361 --> 00:02:49,540
They are then burned for a few minutes to remove dead leaves and a waxy coating.

30
00:02:49,541 --> 00:02:56,160
The fires are quickly extinguished so that the cane and its sugar contents remain unharmed.

31
00:02:56,161 --> 00:03:03,880
The rich soil, abundant sun and water and two-year growth cycle produce weighty stalks

32
00:03:03,881 --> 00:03:05,760
of 120 tons per acre.

33
00:03:05,761 --> 00:03:16,580
We harvest this cane by pushing it into windrows and then load it into 60-ton cane haulers,

34
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:18,180
which then deliver it to the factory.

35
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:24,920
Every 10 pounds of stalks harvested will eventually result in 1 to 1.5 pounds of raw sugar.

36
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:30,720
The HC&S factory has been processing Hawaiian sugarcane since 1903

37
00:03:30,721 --> 00:03:35,800
and now produces over 200,000 tons of sugar every year.

38
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:40,520
As the sugar cane enters the factory,

39
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:43,640
the first part of the process is a cleanup operation.

40
00:03:45,740 --> 00:03:48,840
Soil, rocks, pebbles, and other debris will be washed out

41
00:03:48,841 --> 00:03:50,960
in what's called a density bath.

42
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:55,660
The clean stalks are chopped into fine pieces

43
00:03:55,661 --> 00:03:57,540
by serrated rotating knives.

44
00:03:57,541 --> 00:04:05,260
the pieces then make four passes through a hammer mill where a series of large rollers

45
00:04:05,261 --> 00:04:13,680
crush them even finer separating liquid from fiber eventually 95% of the available sugar

46
00:04:13,681 --> 00:04:21,300
is pressed from the fiber the pressed juice receives an infusion of lime we want to clarify

47
00:04:21,301 --> 00:04:24,900
the juice by getting the suspended solids to settle out.

48
00:04:24,901 --> 00:04:28,500
And the calcium part of the lime helps us to do that.

49
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:33,300
When the calcium bonds to phosphates in the juice,

50
00:04:33,301 --> 00:04:35,900
a gel called calcium phosphate is formed.

51
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:40,700
While the sugar is suspended in solution and unaffected,

52
00:04:40,701 --> 00:04:43,500
heavy non-sugar particles will adhere to the gel

53
00:04:43,501 --> 00:04:45,100
and drop to the bottom of the tank.

54
00:04:45,101 --> 00:04:53,980
The remaining clear juice must still be concentrated. It's boiled five separate

55
00:04:53,981 --> 00:04:58,632
times in an evaporator to remove water from the sugar solution.

56
00:04:58,633 --> 00:05:00,700
Chemically known as sucrose,

57
00:05:00,701 --> 00:05:07,760
the sugar now consists of a thick, super saturated syrup and is ready to form crystals.

58
00:05:07,761 --> 00:05:13,940
We do crystallization here by adding finely divided sucrose crystals about five microns

59
00:05:13,941 --> 00:05:14,755
in diameter.

60
00:05:14,756 --> 00:05:19,780
The sucrose will deposit on the surface of that small crystal and let that

61
00:05:19,781 --> 00:05:23,480
crystal grow in size as more sucrose deposits.

62
00:05:23,481 --> 00:05:32,120
Finally, the mixture is spun in centrifuges at 1,200 revolutions per minute.

63
00:05:32,121 --> 00:05:38,628
During this process, a thick liquid byproduct, known as molasses, is thrown off.

64
00:05:38,629 --> 00:05:39,360
Depending

65
00:05:39,361 --> 00:05:44,540
Depending on the grade of molasses, it can be sold as food, cattle feed, or as a fermentation

66
00:05:44,541 --> 00:05:47,280
source for the rum industry.

67
00:05:47,281 --> 00:05:53,940
The crystals that remain are sugar, which will wind up in your coffee or on your kitchen

68
00:05:53,941 --> 00:05:57,480
table.

69
00:05:57,481 --> 00:06:03,280
Cane extraction has changed little for decades, but advances in technology have increased

70
00:06:03,281 --> 00:06:05,540
factory efficiency.

71
00:06:05,541 --> 00:06:08,500
The biggest change has been the automation of the entire process.

72
00:06:09,500 --> 00:06:17,560
We monitor each machinery with sensors to tell the operator what's going on every moment of the operation.

73
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:20,740
And he can follow it off of computer screens.

74
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:30,800
3,000 miles from Hawaii, in Northern California, sugar is also being grown and processed.

75
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:34,360
Only here, it's coming from sugar beets.

76
00:06:35,540 --> 00:06:41,280
Today's sugar beets account for 35 million metric tons of sugar produced each year,

77
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:44,160
one quarter of global sugar production.

78
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:49,680
While sugar beets and sugar cane are processed differently,

79
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:54,560
the final product is virtually identical, except for varying trace minerals.

80
00:06:55,740 --> 00:07:00,520
There really is no difference in the sugar from either plant species.

81
00:07:00,521 --> 00:07:06,380
The growing of the crop and its extraction are initially quite different,

82
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:09,900
but afterwards the refining process ends up with the same product.

83
00:07:11,380 --> 00:07:14,000
Whereas sugar cane requires tropical weather,

84
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:18,580
sugar beets flourish in the cooler climates of Europe, Asia, and North America.

85
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:25,800
In California's Merced County, farmers grow sugar beets year-round.

86
00:07:25,801 --> 00:07:31,260
We have about 1,700, 1,800 acres of usable soil to farm these sugar beets on.

87
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:34,200
We run about a four-year rotation.

88
00:07:34,600 --> 00:07:37,900
We start with tillage, land planing, prepping the soil,

89
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:42,780
and we put on fertilizer and a herbicide for weeds, plant the seed.

90
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:45,340
The seed comes in a form like this.

91
00:07:45,420 --> 00:07:47,520
It's kind of a special seed.

92
00:07:48,980 --> 00:07:51,440
This seed is developed by companies scientifically

93
00:07:51,441 --> 00:07:57,340
for maximum yield, disease resistance, and also the highest sugar contents possible.

94
00:07:59,340 --> 00:08:02,580
A year after planting, the beets are ready for harvest.

95
00:08:04,100 --> 00:08:08,660
First, a topper uses steel flails to cut them down to their crowns.

96
00:08:10,260 --> 00:08:13,800
Rubber flails immediately follow, knocking the leaf canopy away.

97
00:08:14,420 --> 00:08:18,740
You make several adjustments to your harvester to eliminate any dirt that you can,

98
00:08:18,741 --> 00:08:22,920
because the more dirt you ship, it slows the factory down and causes problems over there.

99
00:08:23,020 --> 00:08:24,880
Plus, it costs the grower money to ship dirt.

100
00:08:26,820 --> 00:08:32,280
Once the leaves are removed, the crop must be collected as soon as possible, usually within a day.

101
00:08:33,220 --> 00:08:36,180
Unless frozen, the beets will remain alive after they're picked,

102
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:39,640
and every hour will consume more of their precious sucrose.

103
00:08:43,580 --> 00:08:47,320
Harvesting machines use sets of pinch wheels to dig underneath the roots,

104
00:08:47,321 --> 00:08:51,080
lifting them from the ground and onto a bed of cleaning rollers.

105
00:08:58,200 --> 00:09:02,180
They are then loaded directly onto a tractor trailer, ready for transport.

106
00:09:04,340 --> 00:09:08,940
What's fun about growing sugar beets is this thing starts with that little seed that we showed earlier,

107
00:09:09,380 --> 00:09:12,700
and it grows into about a 3- or 4-pound beet in about 8- to 10 months.

108
00:09:13,260 --> 00:09:17,000
And this beet is not edible. It has to go through a process to make sugar.

109
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:24,320
In this case, that's the job of the Spreckel Sugar Factory in Mendota, California.

110
00:09:24,321 --> 00:09:31,320
Just a few miles from the beet fields, the factory receives these beets the same day

111
00:09:31,321 --> 00:09:36,320
they're harvested.

112
00:09:36,321 --> 00:09:42,320
The roots are still alive, so the beets must be processed within 48 hours before they burn

113
00:09:42,321 --> 00:09:46,320
up the sugar stored within them.

114
00:09:46,321 --> 00:09:58,080
This factory processes 800,000 tons of beets each year, yielding 200 million pounds of

115
00:09:58,081 --> 00:10:01,080
sugar.

116
00:10:01,081 --> 00:10:07,360
The process begins with a truckload of beets, approximately 25 tons.

117
00:10:07,361 --> 00:10:13,360
Each load is dumped into a hopper and conveyed to a storage pile to be processed during that

118
00:10:13,361 --> 00:10:15,420
day.

119
00:10:15,421 --> 00:10:18,920
The beets fall into a series of flumes.

120
00:10:18,921 --> 00:10:21,460
Rotating water wheels remove dirt and rocks

121
00:10:21,461 --> 00:10:25,960
while simultaneously floating the roots to the slicing center.

122
00:10:25,961 --> 00:10:28,460
They pass through a series of high-speed knives

123
00:10:28,461 --> 00:10:30,160
that cut them into what are called cossets,

124
00:10:30,161 --> 00:10:33,680
which are thin slices.

125
00:10:33,681 --> 00:10:36,440
The thin slices of beets travel in a water bath,

126
00:10:36,441 --> 00:10:38,540
where they're pressed and boiled,

127
00:10:38,541 --> 00:10:40,920
in order to extract a sugar-rich liquid.

128
00:10:40,921 --> 00:10:54,921
From here, the process is virtually the same as that used to extract sugar from sugar cane.

129
00:10:55,980 --> 00:11:01,040
After going through the centrifuge, the remaining sugar crystals are dried in rotating drums

130
00:11:01,041 --> 00:11:07,320
called granulators, but there's still more sugar to be yielded.

131
00:11:07,321 --> 00:11:12,940
The residual liquid from the centrifuge process is molasses, stored in 5,000-ton tanks.

132
00:11:14,540 --> 00:11:20,080
Recently, scientists developed a chemical process that extracts even more sugar from the molasses.

133
00:11:21,760 --> 00:11:26,360
One of the most significant innovations we did was back in 1995,

134
00:11:26,361 --> 00:11:32,640
we installed an ion exclusion process which extracted additional sugar from molasses.

135
00:11:33,420 --> 00:11:36,280
This improved our yield by 25%.

136
00:11:36,281 --> 00:11:43,780
In the ion exclusion process, a resin is added to the molasses, which then coagulates and

137
00:11:43,781 --> 00:11:48,940
forms a glob resembling a ball of yarn in the tank.

138
00:11:48,941 --> 00:11:53,140
The leftover sugar in the molasses enters this web, and the molasses, because of its

139
00:11:53,141 --> 00:12:03,620
particular ion characteristics, is excluded, hence the name ion exclusion.

140
00:12:03,621 --> 00:12:07,260
The extra sugar from this process joins the rest of the sugar in the factory line.

141
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:12,240
Then it passes over screens and goes to our storage silos.

142
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:21,220
From the storage silos, it is either packaged in 50-pound bags or loaded directly into trucks.

143
00:12:26,940 --> 00:12:29,740
Those bags may end up anywhere on Earth.

144
00:12:33,620 --> 00:12:37,200
Sugar has become a global economic powerhouse,

145
00:12:37,380 --> 00:12:40,700
but not until it rose from delicious curiosity

146
00:12:40,701 --> 00:12:44,060
to treasure worth fighting for.

147
00:12:49,340 --> 00:12:53,520
The United States consumes nearly 9 million metric tons

148
00:12:53,521 --> 00:12:55,060
of refined sugar annually.

149
00:12:56,300 --> 00:12:58,840
China, with over four times the population,

150
00:12:59,500 --> 00:13:01,600
consumes just 30% more.

151
00:13:01,601 --> 00:13:05,600
Sugar will return on Modern Marvels.

152
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:15,860
From field to factory,

153
00:13:16,240 --> 00:13:19,040
the process of growing and producing table sugar

154
00:13:19,041 --> 00:13:20,160
is so cost-effective

155
00:13:20,161 --> 00:13:24,300
that the average American can pay for a pound of sugar

156
00:13:24,301 --> 00:13:26,020
with only a few minutes of work.

157
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:30,780
That's a far cry from ancient times

158
00:13:30,781 --> 00:13:33,580
when sweeteners were rare and precious commodities.

159
00:13:34,580 --> 00:13:37,100
We take sweeteners and sugar for granted.

160
00:13:37,740 --> 00:13:40,160
It's so common, but that wasn't always the case.

161
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:45,640
Prior to the widespread availability of sugar and other sweeteners,

162
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:51,740
people got sweet things primarily from honey, from bees, or from fruits.

163
00:13:51,741 --> 00:13:56,220
We all have specific cells in our mouth that respond to sweetness.

164
00:13:56,221 --> 00:14:02,220
and to be able to experience that was a luxury

165
00:14:02,221 --> 00:14:04,220
when it wasn't readily available.

166
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:10,220
The origins of cultivating plants for their sucrose are murky.

167
00:14:10,221 --> 00:14:13,220
The practice probably began in New Guinea

168
00:14:13,221 --> 00:14:17,220
and followed up western path into Asia, India, and the Middle East.

169
00:14:20,220 --> 00:14:23,220
The ancient Chinese developed a technology to extract cane juice

170
00:14:23,221 --> 00:14:28,220
cane juice using two rotating stone mills.

171
00:14:28,221 --> 00:14:34,220
Egyptians discovered that adding lime could remove impurities from the juice.

172
00:14:34,221 --> 00:14:40,220
For centuries, sugar was a secret of the East.

173
00:14:40,221 --> 00:14:47,220
Then, in the 11th century, it was discovered by Western Europeans as a result of the Crusades.

174
00:14:47,221 --> 00:14:51,220
By the 12th century, sugar was worth its weight in gold.

175
00:14:51,221 --> 00:14:56,460
A pound of sugar was so expensive that one Venetian trader reportedly sold 50 tons

176
00:14:56,461 --> 00:14:59,460
for the modern-day equivalent of $10 million.

177
00:15:02,160 --> 00:15:06,500
But sugar's true destiny as an economic powerhouse lay in the New World.

178
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:11,600
Much as oil drives the economy today globally,

179
00:15:12,380 --> 00:15:17,500
sugar and spices drove the economies in the 16th and 17th centuries.

180
00:15:17,501 --> 00:15:22,500
As Europe developed a taste for sugar,

181
00:15:22,501 --> 00:15:26,500
and their explorers were going out throughout the world,

182
00:15:26,501 --> 00:15:30,500
they also moved the sugar crop.

183
00:15:30,501 --> 00:15:34,500
And Christopher Columbus did take this to the Caribbean.

184
00:15:34,501 --> 00:15:39,500
Columbus's mother-in-law had a sugar cane plantation

185
00:15:39,501 --> 00:15:42,500
and gave him cane cuttings to take with him.

186
00:15:42,501 --> 00:15:45,500
Thus, Columbus planted not only a flag,

187
00:15:45,501 --> 00:15:47,260
but an industry in the New World.

188
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:53,700
Europeans found the Caribbean ideal for cultivating sugar.

189
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:58,300
By the 1700s, thousands of flourishing plantations and factories

190
00:15:58,301 --> 00:16:01,840
produced the bulk of the world's sugar on the Caribbean islands.

191
00:16:04,120 --> 00:16:08,140
Sugar was an incredibly valuable commodity in the New World,

192
00:16:08,720 --> 00:16:12,740
and so wherever it grew, whether or not it was Brazil or the Caribbean

193
00:16:12,741 --> 00:16:14,800
or elsewhere in the tropics,

194
00:16:14,801 --> 00:16:18,280
This product was grown for export to Europe.

195
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:25,120
As the trade boomed, an accidental recipe would give the world a new drink

196
00:16:25,121 --> 00:16:26,920
and expand the sugar industry.

197
00:16:27,120 --> 00:16:31,200
Someone one day in Barbados in the 1600s

198
00:16:31,201 --> 00:16:35,500
ended up having wild yeast blow into some diluted molasses

199
00:16:35,501 --> 00:16:37,260
and it produced a beer.

200
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:41,980
That beer was ultimately enjoyed by the local people

201
00:16:41,981 --> 00:16:45,480
and ultimately someone decided to distill it

202
00:16:45,481 --> 00:16:50,440
and created a product that was known locally as rumbolium

203
00:16:50,441 --> 00:16:53,380
because it made people rambunctious

204
00:16:53,381 --> 00:16:57,180
and that was shortened to the word rum eventually.

205
00:16:59,120 --> 00:17:02,140
Rum became the grog of choice for the British Navy.

206
00:17:05,580 --> 00:17:07,460
British sailors roamed the seas

207
00:17:07,461 --> 00:17:10,100
and spread word of their favorite drink to the entire world.

208
00:17:11,980 --> 00:17:18,980
Rum was actually part of their daily rations until 1970.

209
00:17:18,981 --> 00:17:25,980
The increase in demand for sugar and rum sparked a growing need for slaves.

210
00:17:25,981 --> 00:17:31,980
Caribbean plantation owners oversaw a huge workforce of newly imported slave laborers,

211
00:17:31,981 --> 00:17:37,980
shipped from Africa to work the fields in the New World.

212
00:17:37,981 --> 00:17:40,980
Molasses was sent to New England to make rum.

213
00:17:40,981 --> 00:17:44,980
The rum was then sent to Africa, where it was traded for more slaves.

214
00:17:44,981 --> 00:17:50,980
This sugar triangle was a subset of what historians call triangular trade.

215
00:17:53,980 --> 00:17:59,980
By the mid-1700s, sugar had replaced tobacco as the new world's most profitable export.

216
00:17:59,981 --> 00:18:00,980
export.

217
00:18:06,820 --> 00:18:10,100
Sugar and its byproduct, rum, even played a pivotal role

218
00:18:10,101 --> 00:18:11,320
in the American Revolution.

219
00:18:17,940 --> 00:18:21,920
In addition to the tea tax, Britain imposed a molasses tax

220
00:18:21,921 --> 00:18:25,560
and a sugar tax, which infuriated the colonists

221
00:18:25,561 --> 00:18:29,600
and embittered relations with the mother country.

222
00:18:29,601 --> 00:18:31,560
Eventually, there was war.

223
00:18:35,860 --> 00:18:37,500
Eight long years of it.

224
00:18:42,540 --> 00:18:47,340
Britain, like so many countries in Europe, was wild about sugar and keen on pursuing it.

225
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:55,780
Finally, Britain shifted its focus to battling America's ally, France, for the domination of the West Indies sugar trade.

226
00:18:55,781 --> 00:19:03,220
Eventually, America gained its independence when Britain backed off the colonies to concentrate

227
00:19:03,221 --> 00:19:05,500
on its fight over sugar.

228
00:19:05,501 --> 00:19:11,200
The 18th century had seen Caribbean sugar expand into a huge

229
00:19:11,201 --> 00:19:11,901
business.

230
00:19:11,902 --> 00:19:17,980
In the 19th century, Hawaiian growers were independently developing a much smaller

231
00:19:17,981 --> 00:19:20,399
and very different sugar industry.

232
00:19:20,400 --> 00:19:24,740
In Hawaii, natives brought sugar plants from other Polynesian

233
00:19:24,741 --> 00:19:27,560
islands and began their own production.

234
00:19:28,980 --> 00:19:34,940
In the Caribbean, the Europeans came there to essentially exploit the area and bring

235
00:19:34,941 --> 00:19:36,820
everything back to their countries.

236
00:19:37,160 --> 00:19:41,140
Whereas in Hawaii, what happened was Americans came here to settle.

237
00:19:41,600 --> 00:19:47,480
And they reinvested into the economy and the money stayed here.

238
00:19:48,380 --> 00:19:53,400
Long before tourism exploded, sugar cane was the bedrock of the Hawaiian economy.

239
00:19:54,740 --> 00:19:57,740
But globally, sugarcane had a rival.

240
00:19:57,741 --> 00:20:03,740
For thousands of years, sugarcane was the only plant that could produce sucrose in large quantities.

241
00:20:03,741 --> 00:20:11,740
But in the 18th century, two German chemists discovered that the beetroot was also filled with sugar, ripe for the taking.

242
00:20:11,741 --> 00:20:16,740
Andreas Margraf used a water-based extraction process to extract sugar from beets.

243
00:20:16,741 --> 00:20:23,740
His breakthrough inspired fellow German Franz Karl Achard to improve the process.

244
00:20:23,741 --> 00:20:27,520
He used alcohol, which resulted in much greater yields of sugar.

245
00:20:28,840 --> 00:20:35,100
In 1801, Achard opened the world's first sugar beet factory in Lower Silesia, part of present-day Poland.

246
00:20:40,280 --> 00:20:47,180
Just a decade later, Napoleon's epic quest to control Europe would seal the destiny of the lowly beetroot.

247
00:20:47,920 --> 00:20:52,500
Sugar was certainly part of warfare during the Napoleonic era.

248
00:20:53,480 --> 00:20:56,520
The British were able to effectively blockade France

249
00:20:56,521 --> 00:21:00,620
and cut off the trade in sugar from the West Indies.

250
00:21:01,620 --> 00:21:05,800
The French were willing to tolerate a great deal during the Napoleonic era,

251
00:21:05,801 --> 00:21:08,440
but they apparently were not willing to tolerate a lack of sugar.

252
00:21:09,700 --> 00:21:14,400
In 1811, Napoleon ordered 40 sugar beet factories to be constructed.

253
00:21:15,360 --> 00:21:19,080
They were so successful that soon the new product spread across Europe.

254
00:21:19,081 --> 00:21:26,120
By the mid-19th century, innovations were boosting output even further.

255
00:21:27,800 --> 00:21:33,640
In 1843, Norbert Rillieux of Louisiana revolutionized sugar processing.

256
00:21:35,960 --> 00:21:40,560
Rillieux devised a steam heater that evaporated water under controlled repeatable conditions,

257
00:21:41,160 --> 00:21:43,800
producing far drier and purer sugar crystals.

258
00:21:43,801 --> 00:21:49,800
His innovation was quickly followed by improved farming techniques.

259
00:21:49,801 --> 00:21:56,800
Starting in about 1850, a planter was invented that could plant sugar beet seeds in a row.

260
00:21:56,801 --> 00:21:59,982
So all of a sudden, half the seed was needed.

261
00:21:59,983 --> 00:22:03,800
Less seed was needed because you could put it in rows.

262
00:22:03,801 --> 00:22:11,800
And once you could put it in rows, you could begin to use horse-drawn implements

263
00:22:11,801 --> 00:22:15,600
first drawn implements to cultivate between the roads.

264
00:22:15,601 --> 00:22:19,600
And so that labor of weeding could be reduced.

265
00:22:25,700 --> 00:22:28,800
Today, manual labor has been all but eliminated,

266
00:22:28,801 --> 00:22:31,000
further reducing costs.

267
00:22:31,001 --> 00:22:34,400
It's a good thing, because sugar now has thousands

268
00:22:34,401 --> 00:22:37,900
of different applications.

269
00:22:37,901 --> 00:22:40,800
When Franz Karl Achard used alcohol to recover sugar

270
00:22:40,801 --> 00:22:44,760
from beets, his breakthrough process yielded 4% sugar

271
00:22:44,761 --> 00:22:46,220
by weight.

272
00:22:46,221 --> 00:22:51,440
The current standard is between 16% and 18%.

273
00:22:51,441 --> 00:22:53,400
Sugar will return on Modern Marvels.

274
00:22:59,420 --> 00:23:04,380
Sugar is most commonly seen as a key ingredient in baking,

275
00:23:04,381 --> 00:23:07,240
sweetening the cakes and pastries we crave,

276
00:23:07,241 --> 00:23:08,920
and giving flavor to processed foods.

277
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:19,680
Once you get beyond baking, you look at the preservative aspect of sugar used in jams.

278
00:23:19,681 --> 00:23:21,380
You also have candy.

279
00:23:21,381 --> 00:23:24,420
You have beverages that it's used in.

280
00:23:24,421 --> 00:23:27,160
You have ice cream that it's used in.

281
00:23:27,161 --> 00:23:30,640
Sugar provides a little bit of a crispiness in most cereals.

282
00:23:30,641 --> 00:23:33,840
When you add milk, it maintains that crispiness.

283
00:23:33,841 --> 00:23:39,680
The sugar that is standard of trade is pure white, crystalline sugar.

284
00:23:39,681 --> 00:23:44,360
It's highly uniform, whether it's from beet sources or from cane sources.

285
00:23:44,361 --> 00:23:52,620
It's reliably the same in terms of its sweetening capacity and in terms of its baking properties.

286
00:23:52,621 --> 00:23:57,500
Contrary to popular perception, white sugar is an all-natural product.

287
00:23:57,501 --> 00:23:59,860
There's raw sugar and then there's white sugar.

288
00:23:59,861 --> 00:24:04,340
White sugar is basically taking raw sugar and spinning it, sort of like in a washing

289
00:24:04,341 --> 00:24:06,900
machine where you spin the water out of clothes.

290
00:24:06,901 --> 00:24:12,500
basically spin the molasses out of raw sugar to create a white sugar crystal.

291
00:24:14,180 --> 00:24:19,380
Only 10 to 15 percent of sugar consumption is the sugar we sprinkle into our coffee or tea,

292
00:24:19,381 --> 00:24:21,271
or over our cereal.

293
00:24:21,272 --> 00:24:27,940
The remaining 85 to 90 percent comes from the industrial production

294
00:24:27,941 --> 00:24:29,448
of food and drinks.

295
00:24:29,449 --> 00:24:35,320
Even processed fruits and vegetables contain significant amounts of sugar.

296
00:24:36,900 --> 00:24:44,540
When crystallized sugar is dried in a centrifuge, the byproduct is molasses, itself an ingredient

297
00:24:44,541 --> 00:24:47,580
in several types of sugar.

298
00:24:47,581 --> 00:24:52,460
Brown sugar and sugar in the raw share some similarities, as each contains white sugar

299
00:24:52,461 --> 00:24:57,200
combined with 5 to 15 percent molasses.

300
00:24:57,201 --> 00:25:02,100
Cubed sugar is a convenient form for sweetening hot beverages.

301
00:25:02,101 --> 00:25:07,740
Cube sugar is made by a machine that takes fine grains of white refined sugar.

302
00:25:07,741 --> 00:25:13,080
It's misted with the steam or hot water, which causes the crystals to fuse.

303
00:25:13,081 --> 00:25:17,780
And then it's formed up as a cube in a mold or in a press.

304
00:25:17,781 --> 00:25:23,200
Confectioner's sugar is simply sugar that has been ground into a fine powder.

305
00:25:23,201 --> 00:25:29,040
It's a favorite for adding on top of baked goods and donuts.

306
00:25:29,041 --> 00:25:33,640
Candies are created by boiling sugar until the solution forms a non-crystalline glass

307
00:25:33,641 --> 00:25:36,080
structure.

308
00:25:36,081 --> 00:25:41,760
Adding a small amount of corn syrup makes the candy strong enough to maintain its shape.

309
00:25:41,761 --> 00:25:45,540
The sugar industry is constantly looking to new applications.

310
00:25:45,541 --> 00:25:50,000
And now, sugar is increasingly used in cosmetics.

311
00:25:50,001 --> 00:25:55,100
We have body polish, we have body scrub, we have an exfoliant.

312
00:25:55,101 --> 00:26:00,480
Most ladies certainly want to maintain a vibrant and young and youthful look.

313
00:26:00,481 --> 00:26:04,880
And when you can do that, actually new cells, skin cells are produced.

314
00:26:04,881 --> 00:26:11,220
But the sugar basically helps to act as an abrasive and certainly slough off those dead

315
00:26:11,221 --> 00:26:13,880
skin cells.

316
00:26:13,881 --> 00:26:18,720
But for all of sugar's many uses, one of the most popular is as the source material

317
00:26:18,721 --> 00:26:21,080
for rum.

318
00:26:21,081 --> 00:26:29,240
While any type of sugar syrup can be fermented, molasses is the usual source for rum.

319
00:26:29,241 --> 00:26:35,560
Sugar and molasses are separated in a centrifuge by three successive passes, or strikes.

320
00:26:35,561 --> 00:26:41,920
A molasses is the first strike molasses, and that molasses has the highest sugar content

321
00:26:41,921 --> 00:26:45,400
and the least byproducts in the molasses.

322
00:26:45,401 --> 00:26:49,220
C molasses has the most byproducts in it.

323
00:26:49,221 --> 00:26:53,760
So the molasses has a completely different flavor.

324
00:26:53,761 --> 00:26:57,680
Part of rum's unique flavor derives from the surprising amount of sugar contained in its

325
00:26:57,681 --> 00:26:58,680
source.

326
00:26:58,681 --> 00:27:04,300
When we get the molasses, the A molasses looks like this.

327
00:27:04,301 --> 00:27:10,540
It's a thick, viscous liquid that weighs 12 pounds per gallon.

328
00:27:10,541 --> 00:27:14,600
Ten of those pounds are sugar.

329
00:27:14,601 --> 00:27:20,280
rums are made from A-grade molasses, but all rums go through the same process of fermentation.

330
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:25,673
You take the raw molasses, you dilute it in these tanks.

331
00:27:25,674 --> 00:27:28,760
We use these tanks as our fermenters,

332
00:27:28,761 --> 00:27:36,920
and we put in 50 gallons of molasses, add 200 gallons of water, and then we add yeast to the

333
00:27:37,640 --> 00:27:38,685
to the mixture.

334
00:27:38,686 --> 00:27:44,120
During the fermentation process, the yeast consumes the sugar in the molasses.

335
00:27:44,600 --> 00:27:52,600
In doing so, it breaks down the sucrose and glucose molecules into smaller alcohol molecules.

336
00:27:52,601 --> 00:27:55,600
Carbon dioxide is released as a byproduct.

337
00:27:55,601 --> 00:28:07,600
And two days later, voila, the yeast have turned the sugar in the molasses into an alcohol beer of approximately 12% alcohol.

338
00:28:07,601 --> 00:28:13,780
Distilling boosts the beer strength mixture to its full power of approximately 80 proof

339
00:28:13,781 --> 00:28:18,280
alcohol.

340
00:28:18,281 --> 00:28:22,740
Places like the Caribbean that produce the best molasses are generally also home to the

341
00:28:22,741 --> 00:28:26,540
world's finer rums.

342
00:28:26,541 --> 00:28:31,220
Rum has recently grown in popularity and is one of the most important parts of the sugar

343
00:28:31,221 --> 00:28:34,320
business.

344
00:28:34,321 --> 00:28:42,820
But for non-alcoholic soft drinks, there's a formidable challenger to sugar's dominance.

345
00:28:42,821 --> 00:28:50,200
High fructose corn syrup, or HFCS, is so prevalent the average American consumes 65 pounds of

346
00:28:50,201 --> 00:28:52,280
it a year.

347
00:28:52,281 --> 00:28:56,720
While table sugar, chemically known as sucrose, is extracted from cane and beets through a

348
00:28:56,721 --> 00:29:04,360
mechanical process. HFCS is the result of a complex biochemical process.

349
00:29:04,361 --> 00:29:09,540
It's produced by adding to cornstarch a series of enzymes that convert the starch

350
00:29:09,541 --> 00:29:14,560
first into glucose and then into fructose, which are types of sugars.

351
00:29:14,561 --> 00:29:19,440
High fructose corn syrup is made from corn grain. Very large areas of the

352
00:29:19,441 --> 00:29:24,200
Midwest United States is devoted particularly to that crop. So it's

353
00:29:24,201 --> 00:29:28,160
It's abundant in supply and low in cost.

354
00:29:28,161 --> 00:29:33,400
Since the 1980s, major soft drink manufacturers have cut costs by switching from sugar to

355
00:29:33,401 --> 00:29:35,700
HFCS.

356
00:29:35,701 --> 00:29:39,340
So far, Americans have been most accepting of the new taste.

357
00:29:39,341 --> 00:29:44,040
The United States is the major country that high fructose corn syrup has been used by

358
00:29:44,041 --> 00:29:45,580
the soft drink industry.

359
00:29:45,581 --> 00:29:50,740
If you go into Mexico, you immediately notice the difference in taste of soft drinks because

360
00:29:50,741 --> 00:29:54,080
they do use all natural sugar.

361
00:29:54,081 --> 00:29:57,960
There are two battles currently being waged in the world of sugar.

362
00:29:57,961 --> 00:30:04,400
In the US, the sugar industry continues to face the challenge posed by HFCS.

363
00:30:04,401 --> 00:30:13,860
But in Brazil, sugar-based ethanol is poised to strike a knockout punch on the oil industry.

364
00:30:13,861 --> 00:30:19,840
Sugar-sweetened soft drinks contain 10 to 12 teaspoons, or 1.7 ounces of sugar, per

365
00:30:19,841 --> 00:30:22,800
12-ounce can of soda.

366
00:30:22,801 --> 00:30:31,180
Sugar will return on Modern Marvelous.

367
00:30:31,181 --> 00:30:37,160
In the last few decades, science has looked beyond sugar's traditional uses and has been

368
00:30:37,161 --> 00:30:40,520
perfecting a far more potent use of this product.

369
00:30:40,521 --> 00:30:46,980
Catching the industrialized world off guard, this new technology hasn't been fully utilized

370
00:30:46,981 --> 00:30:50,560
by the US, Japan or Europe.

371
00:30:50,561 --> 00:30:55,640
It springs from a nation where the per capita income is just $2,700 per year.

372
00:30:56,760 --> 00:31:02,920
By taking bold and visionary steps, Brazil has vastly reduced its dependency on oil

373
00:31:02,921 --> 00:31:05,980
and is on the verge of becoming energy self-sufficient.

374
00:31:06,840 --> 00:31:13,300
It's a big commitment towards the renewable energy sourcing and the future of our environment.

375
00:31:13,760 --> 00:31:14,480
That's the lesson.

376
00:31:15,760 --> 00:31:19,760
Brazil converts sugar into the alternative motor fuel, ethanol,

377
00:31:19,761 --> 00:31:22,090
which is 90% alcohol.

378
00:31:22,091 --> 00:31:29,300
As the world leader in sugar production, Brazil grows sugar on 15

379
00:31:29,301 --> 00:31:35,376
million acres, double the area of just 20 years ago.

380
00:31:35,377 --> 00:31:40,400
Farmers have to keep up with demand. Almost

381
00:31:40,401 --> 00:31:47,256
half of all new cars today run on mixtures that include some ethanol.

382
00:31:47,257 --> 00:31:49,740
In 1973, Brazil and other

383
00:31:49,741 --> 00:31:54,040
Other oil importing nations faced the shock of the first oil embargo declared by Middle

384
00:31:54,041 --> 00:31:58,280
East suppliers.

385
00:31:58,281 --> 00:32:04,080
Spurred by the crisis, Europe and the United States inched toward energy alternatives.

386
00:32:04,081 --> 00:32:06,360
But Brazil blazed full speed ahead.

387
00:32:06,361 --> 00:32:11,640
So many developments happened since 1973 to today.

388
00:32:11,641 --> 00:32:16,660
I think it was one of the most impressive R&D programs

389
00:32:16,661 --> 00:32:21,200
with practical results ever done by humankind.

390
00:32:21,520 --> 00:32:25,240
Created in 1909, the Model T Ford became the motivation

391
00:32:25,241 --> 00:32:27,280
for one of the great technological advances.

392
00:32:27,400 --> 00:32:31,200
The idea of using ethanol for motor fuel has existed since the early 1900s.

393
00:32:31,860 --> 00:32:33,480
It had to be found to build them faster.

394
00:32:33,560 --> 00:32:36,920
Henry Ford originally planned to run his Model T on biofuels

395
00:32:36,921 --> 00:32:39,440
before turning to cheap and plentiful gasoline.

396
00:32:39,441 --> 00:32:48,280
But Ford sensed that ethanol's time would come, proclaiming it the fuel of the future.

397
00:32:48,281 --> 00:32:51,560
For Brazil, that prediction became reality.

398
00:32:51,561 --> 00:33:01,780
We believe for the 21st century that the petroleum model used in the 20th century has to change.

399
00:33:01,781 --> 00:33:07,560
Brazil has been growing sugarcane since shortly after the Portuguese arrived in 1503.

400
00:33:07,561 --> 00:33:11,320
So it has vast experience.

401
00:33:11,321 --> 00:33:16,920
To produce energy-rich ethanol, which Brazilians simply call alcohol, stalks are processed

402
00:33:16,921 --> 00:33:21,760
and purified like any other sugar cane.

403
00:33:21,761 --> 00:33:26,740
But after the sucrose juice has been extracted, the factory line sends it in two different

404
00:33:26,741 --> 00:33:30,120
directions.

405
00:33:30,121 --> 00:33:36,560
One continues in the traditional path, ultimately winding up as sugar crystals.

406
00:33:36,561 --> 00:33:40,960
But in the second path, the liquid ferments for six hours.

407
00:33:40,961 --> 00:33:44,100
The juice goes to the fermentation tanks.

408
00:33:44,101 --> 00:33:51,600
Then we throw more or less 70% juice and the other 30% yeast.

409
00:33:51,601 --> 00:33:56,840
This yeast will transform the sucrose from the juice into ethanol.

410
00:33:56,841 --> 00:34:01,000
The ethanol process is just a slight variation of the rum process.

411
00:34:01,001 --> 00:34:06,160
It's based on fermentation and distillation.

412
00:34:06,161 --> 00:34:13,240
This single factory can process 900,000 liters of ethanol each day, enough to fill up 20,000

413
00:34:13,241 --> 00:34:17,200
car tanks.

414
00:34:17,201 --> 00:34:24,940
Brazil produces 35% of the world's ethanol, five times more than the United States.

415
00:34:24,941 --> 00:34:31,200
In the U.S., ethanol blends account for 30% of all motor fuels.

416
00:34:31,201 --> 00:34:39,920
While most U.S. ethanol is made from corn, Brazil's output is entirely sugar-based.

417
00:34:39,921 --> 00:34:44,600
In conjunction with automakers, Brazil has also encouraged the development of flex-fuel

418
00:34:44,601 --> 00:34:55,980
cars, vehicles able to run on ethanol, gasoline, or any mixture of the two.

419
00:34:55,981 --> 00:35:01,780
As fuel is burned in a flex fuel engine, an oxygen sensor measures oxygen content, which

420
00:35:01,781 --> 00:35:04,720
reveals the composition of the fuel.

421
00:35:04,721 --> 00:35:11,140
For each fuel mixture, there are specific injector and spark settings to maximize performance.

422
00:35:11,141 --> 00:35:16,280
Once the sensor recognizes that a new fuel is coming in, the controller of the engine,

423
00:35:16,281 --> 00:35:22,920
which is a small computer, will process all the information to the engine and will regulate

424
00:35:22,921 --> 00:35:27,420
the engine to the new mixture that is coming in.

425
00:35:27,421 --> 00:35:33,900
General Motors' 1.8-liter engine control module adjusts to the fuel mixture in real-time,

426
00:35:33,901 --> 00:35:44,100
with a maximum output of 109 horsepower on straight ethanol and 105 horsepower on gasoline.

427
00:35:44,101 --> 00:35:49,720
The engine is the heart of GM's Corsa FlexPower subcompact, the company's flagship vehicle

428
00:35:49,721 --> 00:35:51,960
in this category.

429
00:35:51,961 --> 00:35:57,880
It's one of many flex fuel cars available for Brazilian drivers.

430
00:35:57,881 --> 00:36:02,520
Numerous car manufacturers have been setting up plants here, eager to build the next generation

431
00:36:02,521 --> 00:36:08,900
of vehicles, all because of the energy contained in sugar.

432
00:36:08,901 --> 00:36:12,840
Brazilian motorists embracing this technology have no problem finding alcohol to fill their

433
00:36:12,841 --> 00:36:14,840
tanks.

434
00:36:14,841 --> 00:36:17,100
In Brazil, we have the distribution.

435
00:36:17,101 --> 00:36:20,140
We have alcohol in every gas station.

436
00:36:20,141 --> 00:36:27,420
By a law from the 80s, that every gas station in Brazil should have all the fuels, gasoline,

437
00:36:27,421 --> 00:36:30,900
alcohol and diesel.

438
00:36:30,901 --> 00:36:36,780
As for the environmental impact, car engines pollute whether they run on gas or ethanol.

439
00:36:36,781 --> 00:36:41,700
With gasoline, the main culprits are carbon monoxide and dioxide.

440
00:36:41,701 --> 00:36:44,860
With ethanol, it's nitrogen oxide.

441
00:36:44,861 --> 00:36:51,140
An environmental benefit comes from the fact that producing biofuels is a far cleaner procedure

442
00:36:51,141 --> 00:36:53,040
than refining oil.

443
00:36:53,041 --> 00:37:00,120
Also, a car burning 100% sugar-derived ethanol generates the same amount of carbon dioxide

444
00:37:00,121 --> 00:37:03,620
as would have been released by the sugar plants as they decayed.

445
00:37:03,621 --> 00:37:11,020
Thus, to environmentalists, the process is considered carbon neutral.

446
00:37:11,021 --> 00:37:14,720
Even the major oil companies have adapted to the new realities of the Brazilian energy

447
00:37:14,721 --> 00:37:15,720
market.

448
00:37:15,721 --> 00:37:23,400
We are happy to see big names like the oil companies jumping into the program and helping,

449
00:37:23,401 --> 00:37:29,540
because we need their logistics, we need their distribution course.

450
00:37:29,541 --> 00:37:34,080
In recent years, the average cost of filling up at a Sao Paulo gas station has dropped

451
00:37:34,081 --> 00:37:39,360
from 40 US dollars for a tank of gasoline to less than half of that for a tank of ethanol.

452
00:37:57,360 --> 00:38:02,440
Ethanol, which has a higher boiling point than motor fuel, becomes less combustible in cold weather.

453
00:38:04,080 --> 00:38:08,800
So most flex-fuel cars have added a small extra tank to hold just gasoline.

454
00:38:10,580 --> 00:38:13,800
Drivers can thus use gasoline to get going on chilly mornings

455
00:38:13,801 --> 00:38:18,040
and afterwards run on ethanol once the engine is warmed up.

456
00:38:19,440 --> 00:38:22,020
Western countries are now taking a fresh look at ethanol,

457
00:38:22,820 --> 00:38:25,240
seeking their share of the Brazilian energy miracle.

458
00:38:26,600 --> 00:38:32,340
The U.S. Congress's 2005 energy bill mandates doubling ethanol production by 2112.

459
00:38:32,341 --> 00:38:37,340
But this is a far cry from Brazil's track record.

460
00:38:37,341 --> 00:38:44,340
They've gone from roughly an 80% dependency on foreign oil to roughly a 10% to 15% dependency.

461
00:38:44,341 --> 00:38:52,340
We think the use of sugar in the future is obviously going to be an important component of renewable fuels for the United States.

462
00:38:52,341 --> 00:38:59,340
FlexFuel is here to stay. Our cars from now on, every new development is flex.

463
00:38:59,341 --> 00:39:06,340
We see that 100% of our production will be flex cars, and it's growing very fast.

464
00:39:06,341 --> 00:39:11,340
And I believe in the United States, with the highest education standard in the population,

465
00:39:11,341 --> 00:39:14,340
it will be even easier than here.

466
00:39:14,341 --> 00:39:21,340
Brazil has clearly shown the world how the sugar industry can make a positive contribution to a nation's energy needs.

467
00:39:21,341 --> 00:39:27,340
In another part of the world, Hawaiian sugar producers have been providing electrical power

468
00:39:27,341 --> 00:39:33,140
electrical power for nearly 100 years.

469
00:39:33,141 --> 00:39:37,640
In 2004, Brazilian refiners made 4 billion gallons of alcohol

470
00:39:37,641 --> 00:39:43,140
fuel, enough to replace 460 million barrels of oil.

471
00:39:43,141 --> 00:39:46,940
Sugar will return on Modern Marvels,

472
00:39:46,941 --> 00:39:48,540
here on the History Channel.

473
00:39:57,340 --> 00:40:03,220
Brazil's use of ethanol to power cars has fundamentally changed the economy and future prospects of that country.

474
00:40:06,260 --> 00:40:12,560
In Hawaii, sugar used to create a different kind of energy has played a critical role for the past century.

475
00:40:16,200 --> 00:40:22,660
Today, factories like this one, run by the Hawaiian Commercial and Sugar Company, generate their own power.

476
00:40:27,340 --> 00:40:30,480
When shredded cane is crushed by rollers to extract sucrose,

477
00:40:31,060 --> 00:40:35,020
the remaining fiber, known as bagasse, acts as a source of power.

478
00:40:36,740 --> 00:40:40,720
The bagasse, which is the fibrous residue from the cane plant,

479
00:40:41,180 --> 00:40:47,380
becomes our main fuel for generating steam and electricity for operating our factory,

480
00:40:48,240 --> 00:40:50,980
as well as our irrigation systems on the farm.

481
00:40:52,680 --> 00:40:56,860
The bagasse is burned in boilers, creating two types of energy.

482
00:40:56,861 --> 00:41:04,440
Co-generation is a system where we first generate steam in a boiler to provide energy to the process.

483
00:41:04,580 --> 00:41:08,360
But before we use the steam, we also use it to generate electricity.

484
00:41:09,240 --> 00:41:11,480
So we have two forms of energy available.

485
00:41:13,440 --> 00:41:20,520
Each year, more than 600,000 tons of bagasse are burned here, providing more energy than the factory can use.

486
00:41:20,521 --> 00:41:32,280
HC&S generates 30 megawatts and exports 12 megawatts to the local utility company, Maui Electric, supplying 7% of Maui's electricity.

487
00:41:36,080 --> 00:41:40,380
At one time, the sugar company provided for all of Maui's electricity needs.

488
00:41:40,381 --> 00:41:47,440
The relationship really began a long time ago when Maui Electric, as we know it, was formed back in 1921.

489
00:41:47,441 --> 00:41:54,260
At that time, essentially, Maui Electric was an electrical distribution company

490
00:41:54,261 --> 00:41:58,860
with all of the electricity coming from HC&S.

491
00:42:00,640 --> 00:42:03,780
During the dark days of the Depression and World War II,

492
00:42:04,140 --> 00:42:08,500
with demand for electricity soaring, HC&S kept Maui running.

493
00:42:09,620 --> 00:42:14,520
Pearl Harbor, World War II, that increased the demand for electricity on the island.

494
00:42:14,521 --> 00:42:19,440
And at that point, all of the electricity really came from the sugar plantation.

495
00:42:22,320 --> 00:42:25,620
With today's uncertainty surrounding oil-based fuels,

496
00:42:26,140 --> 00:42:29,940
Hawaiian sugar growers are poised to extract still more energy from the sugar cane.

497
00:42:34,360 --> 00:42:37,740
But the Hawaiian sugar industry isn't stopping with energy.

498
00:42:38,680 --> 00:42:42,080
Scientists are researching the next frontier in sugar-based products.

499
00:42:42,081 --> 00:42:47,080
They envision a future where sugar is used to produce pharmaceuticals.

500
00:42:47,081 --> 00:42:50,080
Pharmaceuticals are extremely caustic.

501
00:42:50,081 --> 00:42:57,080
We already know that the production process in the field is much cheaper than the production process in a factory.

502
00:42:57,081 --> 00:43:03,080
Experiments are being conducted in which human DNA is injected into sugar cane.

503
00:43:03,081 --> 00:43:10,080
The plant then acts as a host and can actually grow human protein.

504
00:43:10,081 --> 00:43:17,800
It is also safer, because what they do now is use animal cells that can carry viruses

505
00:43:17,801 --> 00:43:24,560
and creating these proteins that are new medicines, whereas any plant virus will not carry over

506
00:43:24,561 --> 00:43:25,560
to people.

507
00:43:25,561 --> 00:43:27,580
Therefore, it is a safer mechanism.

508
00:43:27,581 --> 00:43:29,900
So we're very excited about the potential.

509
00:43:29,901 --> 00:43:36,640
Sugar, which for so long has given pleasure to humans, may hold a key to extending human

510
00:43:36,641 --> 00:43:37,640
life.

511
00:43:37,641 --> 00:43:41,640
I feel we have a very viable option for the Hawaiian sugar industry.

512
00:43:41,641 --> 00:43:44,640
It's going to take decades, just like all research does,

513
00:43:44,641 --> 00:43:47,640
but we're starting the process now.

514
00:43:49,640 --> 00:43:52,640
In its long history, sugar has demonstrated its power,

515
00:43:52,641 --> 00:43:55,640
playing a role in everything from war and slavery

516
00:43:55,641 --> 00:43:58,640
to tooth decay and obesity.

517
00:43:58,641 --> 00:44:02,640
As a food, it has provided pleasure and energy to humankind.

518
00:44:02,641 --> 00:44:06,640
As a resource, it has provided power,

519
00:44:06,641 --> 00:44:11,020
lighting up our cities and supplying fuel to a growing number of cars.

520
00:44:11,620 --> 00:44:14,840
While oil may have been the driving force of the 20th century,

521
00:44:15,460 --> 00:44:20,620
there are many who believe that sugar will sweeten our options in the 21st.

